## 2. Objective & success definition

- **Objective:** reduce the time an OPD consultant needs to reach important patient information during busy periods, with zero added data-entry burden and no new patient-safety risk.
- **Primary success metric:** median time-to-needed-info ≥50% below a measured baseline `[P]` (baseline established in the validation sprint; target confirmed after baseline).
- **Guardrail metrics:** 0 wrong-patient opens; 100% source-attribution audit pass; 100% access/denial logging.
- **Non-goals:** diagnosis, clinical alerts or decision support, data entry/editing, AI inference, external ABDM records (phase 2).

## 3. Users & primary flow

Primary user: OPD consultant. Secondary: nurse, front desk, duty doctor. Core flow: **(1)** search patient → **(2)** confirm identity (mandatory when ambiguous) → **(3)** review 4-panel summary → **(4)** act or open source record → **(5)** return to HMIS. Design principle: the dashboard complements the HMIS; it never becomes a second system of record.

## 4. Scope — the four panels (read-only, IPS-conformant)

| Panel | Contents | IPS / standard mapping |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Appointments | Today's / relevant scheduling context for the patient | ABDM appointment/encounter context |
| 2. Basic history | Active problems, allergies/intolerances, current medications, last encounter summary | IPS required: Problem List, Allergies, Medication Summary |
| 3. Reports | Recent lab / investigation / imaging results with date & source | IPS recommended: Results; LOINC-coded where available |
| 4. Pending actions | The four pending-access states (MRD §5), plus items awaiting review | Product-specific; consent state per ABDM |

